<figure class="return-boundary" data-return-boundary aria-labelledby="r43-return-title">
 <p class="figure-title" id="r43-return-title">Figure 1 · The old files can exist and still be the wrong return</p>
 <p>Conceptual copy-mode upgrade. The shop rows match; an anonymous request probe makes the new history visible.</p>
 <label>Inspection point <select data-point><option value="before">Before accepting new writes</option><option value="after">After accepting a new write</option></select></label>
 <div class="histories"><div><strong>Preserved PostgreSQL 17 copy</strong><p>Orders 101–103 intact</p><p data-old data-live>Probe: before-upgrade</p><p class="gate">Write gate: closed</p></div><div class="transfer" aria-hidden="true">→ copy upgrade →</div><div><strong>PostgreSQL 18 candidate</strong><p>Orders 101–103 intact</p><p data-new data-live>Probe: before-upgrade</p><p data-gate data-live>Write gate: closed</p></div></div>
 <p data-explanation data-live aria-live="polite">Before new writes, both copies contain the same accepted history. A planned return can choose the preserved old copy after closing the candidate and checking the chosen transfer mode.</p>
 <figcaption>Change the inspection point. A new accepted request changes the return problem even when every original shop row still compares equal. Link mode has an earlier physical boundary: starting the new cluster can modify files shared with the old one.</figcaption>
 <details><summary>Read the return boundary as text</summary><p>Copy mode preserves separate old files. Before new writes, accepted histories can match. After the new authority accepts after-cutover, the old copy lacks that request. Routing back then loses accepted work unless a tested reverse transfer or reconciliation preserves it. The figure keeps the old writer fenced in both cases.</p></details>
</figure>
<style>.return-boundary{padding:1rem;border:1px solid var(--color-rule-strong);margin:2rem 0;background:var(--color-surface-alt);font:.9rem/1.55 var(--font-sans)}.return-boundary .figure-title{margin:0 0 1rem;font-size:1.1rem}.histories{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr auto 1fr;gap:.5rem;align-items:center;margin:1rem 0}.histories>div:not(.transfer){border:1px solid var(--color-text-secondary);padding:.7rem;min-width:0}.histories p{margin:.6rem 0 0}.transfer{color:var(--color-accent);font-size:.8rem}.gate{font-weight:600}select{max-width:100%;font:inherit;background:var(--color-surface);color:var(--color-text);padding:.4rem;border:1px solid var(--color-text-secondary)}select:focus-visible{outline:2px solid var(--color-accent);outline-offset:3px}figcaption{color:var(--color-text-secondary);font-size:.85rem;margin:1rem 0}details p{margin-top:.8rem}@media(max-width:600px){.histories{grid-template-columns:1fr}.transfer{text-align:center}}</style>
<script>
for(const root of document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-return-boundary]')){
 const select=root.querySelector<HTMLSelectElement>('[data-point]')!;
 select.addEventListener('change',()=>{const after=select.value==='after';root.querySelector('[data-new]')!.textContent=after?'Probes: before-upgrade + after-cutover':'Probe: before-upgrade';root.querySelector('[data-gate]')!.textContent=after?'Write gate: new authority open':'Write gate: closed';root.querySelector('[data-explanation]')!.textContent=after?'The old copy still starts and its shop rows still match. But it lacks after-cutover, which the new authority accepted. Returning traffic now requires preserving that new work, not merely restarting the old binaries.':'Before new writes, both copies contain the same accepted history. A planned return can choose the preserved old copy after closing the candidate and checking the chosen transfer mode.';});
}
</script>
